+print(f"candidate_result={candidate(values)}")

codex
The bridge audit found a precise limitation rather than a false rule: all three bridge-free claims over the candidate’s exact symbolic `Val + isInt` match domain get stuck, while bridge-free typed-`Int` claims with arbitrary continuations all close, ground projections 3→3 and 4→4 close, and the opposite 3→4 is rejected. Thus every realizable integer match behaves correctly, but the candidate lacks a machine-checked theorem connecting the broader symbolic pattern to that typed domain. I’m preserving this as a trust/evidence concern and proceeding to the independent false-result mutation.
Claims were restated and witnessed; the closure body was mechanically pinned to the parsed MPY; a ground entry returned 12 in K and both Python implementations. The complete 949-record inventory and bridge-specific fixed-semantics experiments are complete.
